## 1. Introduction

### 1.1 Purpose

The PRD defines **what** must be built. This TRD defines **how**: the architecture, the technologies, exactly how the reused NIDS backend and the other repositories are used, the data formats exchanged between roles, the folder structure that prevents merge conflicts, and the technical design of every module. The four build guides turn this design into step-by-step instructions.

### 1.2 What Changed in Version 1.1

Version 1.0 planned a new FastAPI backend that borrowed ideas from `123dars/NIDS`. The team decided instead that **the backend is taken completely from NIDS**, with no rewrite in another framework. This version is rewritten around that decision. The other consequences are:

| Change | Reason |
|---|---|
| Flask (NIDS) replaces FastAPI | The backend is NIDS as it is |
| Polling replaces WebSocket | NIDS has no WebSocket or push channel |
| SQLite replaces PostgreSQL, and Docker is not used | NIDS reads its database address from a `DATABASE_URL` setting (to verify); Docker is heavy on modest Windows laptops |
| `bqmxnh/ids-agent` is dropped | It is Linux and live-capture only, and NIDS already has its own ingestion |
| The web app is served by the Flask app | One deployment gives one HTTPS address for the phone |
| Detection has two parts | NIDS's detector runs the live demo; a CICIDS2017 model is trained offline to give the evaluation numbers |

### 1.2b What Changed in Version 1.2

While building and testing LEAD-CORE build guide Part 1, several "To verify" items were checked, and some earlier statements turned out to be wrong. This version corrects them:

| # | Correction |
