## 2. Technical Overview and Design Principles

### 2.1 Design Principles

| # | Principle | Consequence |
|---|---|---|
| **P1** | **Replay, not live.** No live capture, no live attack, no real enforcement | All traffic is scripted or from a PCAP file; containment is a status change plus a log entry |
| **P2** | **Use NIDS as it is.** Extend it; do not rewrite it | Add-on files register extra routes; changes to NIDS's own files are limited to the short list in Section 10.2 |
| **P3** | **Contract first.** Data formats between roles are defined before anyone builds | Section 12; each role builds against sample JSON |
| **P4** | **One folder, one owner.** Each person edits only their own folder | Section 6 |
| **P5** | **Only the lead merges** | Branch protection and CODEOWNERS |
| **P6** | **Every risky part has a switch and a fallback** | Section 18 |
| **P7** | **Deterministic demo** | Seeded randomness, fixed scenario files, no network dependency on localhost |
| **P8** | **Honest evaluation** | Reported metrics come from held-out data, never from the rehearsed golden path (Section 9.9) |
| **P9** | **Low hardware demand** | No GPU, no Docker, no database server, no live packet capture |
| **P10** | **No fake intelligence presented as real** | NIDS's threat-actor names and location data are simulated; the product never shows them as real intelligence (Section 15) |

### 2.2 Key Design Decisions

| ID | Decision | Reasoning |
|---|---|---|
| **DD-1** | The backend is the NIDS Flask app, used as-is, plus **add-on files** that register extra routes through one Flask blueprint | The team's decision; avoids a rewrite |
| **DD-2** | NIDS provides: users and login, the packet table, the detector, PCAP analysis, its own dashboard pages, statistics and an HTML report page. **The add-ons provide everything else**: replay, security events, attack graph, Precog, containment, session record, polling state, push and serving the web app | NIDS has no graph, forecast, containment or live push (Section 5.1) |
| **DD-3** | Add-ons run **in the same process** as NIDS and share its database connection; product state that NIDS has no table for (graph, forecasts, containment, session) is held in memory | Keeps deployment to one process; matches the PRD's no-long-term-retention rule |
| **DD-4** | Live updates use **polling with a version number**: clients ask for changes since the last version they saw | NIDS has no push channel; a version number makes each poll cheap |
| **DD-5** | Scripted traffic is written into the NIDS packet table **unlabelled**, and NIDS's own detector function is **called immediately** afterwards instead of waiting for its 30-second loop | The real detector labels the packets, and the latency target is met |
| **DD-6** | Scripted **technique events** are injected directly as security events | Lets the golden path name ATT&CK techniques, which NIDS's packet-level detector cannot |
| **DD-7** | The CICIDS2017 model is trained **offline in DETECT's own environment** and is never imported by the backend | Avoids library clashes with NIDS's pinned versions; produces the evaluation numbers the paper needs |
| **DD-8** | The live attack graph is a `networkx` graph; `cwccie/attackgraph` supplies the static topology, feasible paths and ATT&CK-mapped risk | `attackgraph` is static (Section 5.4) |
| **DD-9** | Precog has two layers: a node-level Markov model (Top-3 hosts) and a technique-level hint exported once from the reused repository's Markov part into a JSON file | The reused repository predicts techniques, not hosts, and needs PyTorch and Python 3.11 (Section 5.5) |
| **DD-10** | Containment is a two-step, confirmed action: **propose**, then **confirm** | Implements human confirmation in the API, so no client can skip it |
| **DD-11** | The web project is split into per-role areas with placeholder files created first by LEAD-CORE | Prevents merge conflicts in the shared project |
| **DD-12** | The web app is served by the Flask app under the path `/app/`, leaving NIDS's own pages untouched | One deployment; same-origin cookies work without extra settings |
| **DD-13** | The web clients log in through an add-on JSON login that uses NIDS's own user table and login system | NIDS's own login is a page that redirects, which a web app cannot use cleanly; the add-on reuses the same users and the same session cookie |

