### 5.3 `Western-OC2-Lab/Intrusion-Detection-System-Using-Machine-Learning` (offline CICIDS2017 model)

| Aspect | Finding (Verified from README) |
|---|---|
| What it is | Research code behind published papers: tree-based models (decision tree, random forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, CatBoost), k-means, stacking and ensembles, Bayesian hyperparameter optimization |
| **How it is used (DD-7)** | As the training code for DETECT's **offline** CICIDS2017 model, run in DETECT's own environment, to produce the precision, recall, F1 and false-positive-rate numbers for the paper |
| To verify | The dataset format it expects and the training time on a modest laptop; the fallback is to use CICIDS2017's own labels for the evaluation |

### 5.4 `cwccie/attackgraph` (static attack graph)

| Aspect | Finding (Verified from README) |
|---|---|
| What it is | A Python package that builds attack graphs from network topology plus vulnerability data, works out feasible attack paths, maps steps to MITRE ATT&CK, and scores risk. **`pip install attackgraph` does NOT work** (the package is not on PyPI); install from GitHub at commit `cfeb580801e1ad19625a57e038612365248b4548` |
| Fit problem | It builds a **static** graph; it does not turn observed events into a live graph |
| **How it is used (DD-8)** | To describe the demo network, compute feasible paths and supply ATT&CK-mapped risk. The live graph (observed connections, compromised and contained states) is a `networkx` graph updated from events. Feasible paths also restrict which hosts Precog may forecast |

### 5.5 `mayank02raj/MITRE-ATTACK-based-Attack-Chain-Prediction` (technique-level Precog layer)

| Aspect | Finding (Verified from README) |
|---|---|
| What it is | A hybrid LSTM and Markov framework forecasting the next ATT&CK **technique**, with beam search and a 0 to 10 risk score. Linked to a SECRYPT 2026 paper |
| Prediction target | The next **technique**, not the next **host** |
| Licence | **Pending institutional review**; the README asks users to contact the author before using or building on the code. The team has the author's permission by email |
| Requirements | Python 3.11 or later and PyTorch; CUDA recommended |
| Cost | LSTM training took about 3.1 hours on an RTX 4090; the Markov model trains in under a second; inference is under 0.2 seconds per prefix |
| Stated extension ideas | Streaming pipelines and kill-chain interruption, which is close in spirit to this product |
| **How it is used (DD-9)** | CPU only, in its own environment, **once**: export its Markov transition table to a JSON file. The backend only reads that file. The LSTM is not trained |
| Obligations | Keep the permission email; cite the repository and its paper; do not redistribute its code outside the project without the author's agreement |
| **Novelty note** | Technique-level attack-chain forecasting already exists here. The product's contribution is **node-level next-host forecasting combined with confirmed, closed-loop, pre-emptive containment under replay**. The paper's novelty claim must be worded to that narrower contribution, and the literature search (PRD Section 10.3) must cover this prior work |
| To verify | Whether the Markov export runs without importing PyTorch; whether trained files are included |

