### 6.2 Web Project Structure (Conflict-Free by Design)

```text
web/
├── package.json, vite config, index.html, manifest, service-worker setup   LEAD-CORE
└── src/
    ├── App and router                    LEAD-CORE   (written once; never edited by others)
    ├── shared/                           LEAD-CORE   (API client, polling hook, types, theme)
    └── areas/
        ├── mobile/                       GRAPH       (all phone-app screens)
        ├── console/                      VIZ         (3D console)
        ├── charts/                       VIZ         (Chart.js components)
        └── push/                         VIZ         (Firebase setup, notification handling)
```

**How merge conflicts are avoided (DD-11):** LEAD-CORE creates the whole structure first with **placeholder files** in every area and merges it before anyone starts. Each person replaces only their own placeholders. `App` and the router import each area through one fixed entry file that already exists, so nobody edits `App`. A component another role needs (for example a chart shown on a mobile screen) is exposed through a placeholder with fixed properties matching a contract.

### 6.3 Branches and Merging

| Item | Rule |
|---|---|
| Branches | `main` plus one working branch per person, named after the alias (for example `detect`) |
| Direct work on `main` | Not allowed for anyone |
| Merge route | A member commits to their own branch, notifies LEAD-CORE, and LEAD-CORE merges through a Pull Request |
| After a merge | Everyone brings `main` into their own branch |
| Branch protection | Require a Pull Request; restrict merging to LEAD-CORE; block direct pushes |
| `CODEOWNERS` | One line: `* @<lead's GitHub username>`, so every pull request needs the lead's approval. Folder ownership is by convention and is checked by the lead in the merge routine |
| Contracts | Change only through LEAD-CORE's merge, and LEAD-CORE tells everyone |

