## 9. Precog Engine Design

### 9.1 Two Layers (DD-9)

| Layer | Question answered | Source | Output |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Node-level** | Which host will the attacker try next? | The product's own Markov model over hosts | Top-3 hosts with confidence |
| **Technique-level** | Which ATT&CK technique is likely next? | The reused repository's Markov table, exported once to a JSON file | A "likely next technique" hint |

### 9.2 Node-Level Model

**State:** the currently compromised host. **Candidates:** hosts reachable from it according to the feasible paths, excluding hosts already compromised or contained.

```text
P(j | i) = ( count(i -> j) + alpha ) / ( sum over candidates k of count(i -> k) + alpha * number_of_candidates )
```

`alpha` is a small smoothing constant (Proposed starting value 0.1, tuned on the training split). The three highest probabilities form the Top-3, shown as a percentage and as cone strength. If fewer than three candidates remain, fewer are shown.

**Optional (Could):** candidates whose services match the technique hint may be re-weighted. The plain model satisfies the Must requirements.

### 9.3 Technique-Level Layer

One time, in its own environment, the reused repository's Markov part is run and its transition table is saved as a JSON file in `lead-core/precog`. The backend reads that file; it never imports the repository. If the export fails, the fallback is a **rule-based ATT&CK lookup table** (`FORECAST_SOURCE=lookup`). The forecast records which source produced it.

### 9.4 When a Forecast Is Produced and Updated

| Trigger | Action |
|---|---|
| A host becomes compromised | Compute and publish a forecast |
| New evidence arrives | Recompute and replace the pending forecast |
| The replay restarts | Clear pending forecasts |

### 9.5 Resolving a Forecast

A forecast is resolved when the attacker's **next attempted move** is observed (an attempt event, successful or blocked).

| Outcome | Rule |
|---|---|
| **Hit at rank r** | The attempted target is the forecast's r-th host (1 to 3) |
| **Miss** | The attempted target is not in the Top-3 |
| **Blocked hit** | The attempted target was predicted and was contained: the prediction was right and the attack was stopped before it succeeded |

### 9.6 Confidence

Confidence is the model's probability (0 to 1), shown as a percentage. It is called **calibrated** only as far as the calibration curve shows.

### 9.7 Training Data

| Item | Design |
|---|---|
| Source | The seeded scenario generator (Section 7.5) |
| Size | Proposed: 300 generated sequences, 80% for training and 20% held out |
| Variety | Several attacker strategy profiles |
| Reproducibility | The seed and settings are stored with the model file |

### 9.8 Evaluation (PRD EV-6 to EV-10)

| Metric | Definition |
|---|---|
| precision@1 | Share of held-out forecasts where the next attempted host was the top-ranked host |
| precision@3 | Share where it was anywhere in the Top-3 |
| Calibration | Group forecasts into confidence bins of width 0.1; compare each bin's average stated confidence with its observed hit rate |
| Containment success rate | Of held-out scenarios where Contain was applied to a predicted host, the share in which the chain ended early |
| Baseline | The same metrics for the rule-based lookup |

### 9.9 Integrity Note

The golden path is a **scripted demonstration** whose forecast is arranged to be correct. Metrics reported in the paper and as results come only from the **held-out generated sequences**, which include wrong forecasts. The live counter shown in the demo is labelled as the session's counter, not the model's overall accuracy.

