## 10. Backend Design (NIDS plus Add-Ons)

### 10.1 How NIDS Is Included

An unchanged copy of NIDS is placed in `lead-core/backend/nids/` at a recorded upstream commit, with its MIT licence file and copyright notice kept. Our own code lives beside it in `lead-core/backend/addons/`, never inside NIDS's folders.

### 10.2 Changes to NIDS Files: None

**No NIDS file is edited.** The start-up script `lead-core/backend/run_app.py` imports NIDS's Flask app from the outside and adjusts it:

| What is adjusted | How, without editing NIDS |
|---|---|
| The hard-coded secret key | `run_app.py` sets `app.secret_key` from the `SECRET_KEY` setting after importing NIDS |
| The default admin password | `run_app.py` writes the hash of `ADMIN_PASSWORD` into NIDS's own `users` table before serving, so NIDS's default password never works |
| Cookie settings | `run_app.py` sets `HttpOnly`, `SameSite=Lax` and, when hosted, `Secure` |
| The database | `DATABASE_URL` is set before NIDS is imported (NIDS reads it at import time) |
| NIDS's relative paths (`../data/...`) | `run_app.py` changes into NIDS's dashboard folder before importing and stays there |
| The detector's model file | `run_app.py` trains NIDS's model with NIDS's own training function when the file is missing |
| Our routes | `run_app.py` registers our blueprint through `addons.register_addons(...)` |

NIDS's `run.py` is never used: it starts Flask in debug mode.

### 10.3 Add-On Structure (`lead-core/backend/addons`)

| Part | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Loader | Adds role folders to Python's search path and registers the blueprint |
| Blueprint (`/api/x`) | All new routes (Section 11) |
| World state | One in-memory object: live graph, alerts, forecasts, containment actions, replay status, session record, and a **version number** that goes up on every change |
| Pipeline runner | Called by the replay controller; runs event processing, graph update and Precog, then bumps the version |
| Web app server | Serves the built web app under `/app/` (DD-12) |
| Push sender | Sends Firebase notifications for high-severity alerts |
| Sample mode | Loads the sample files from `contracts/samples` so clients can run without the pipeline (`DATA_MODE=sample`) |

### 10.4 Concurrency

One Flask process with one worker (state is in memory). The replay controller runs as a background thread (the web server is not asynchronous). State changes are applied one at a time, each followed by a version bump.

### 10.5 Polling Protocol (DD-4)

| Step | Behaviour |
|---|---|
| 1 | The client calls `GET /api/x/state?since=N`, where N is the last version it saw (0 on first call) |
| 2 | If nothing changed, the server replies with the current version and `changed: false` (very small) |
| 3 | If something changed, the server replies with the new version and the full current state |
| Interval | 1 to 2 seconds (Proposed 1.5) |
| Stale marker | If no successful reply arrives for twice the interval, the client marks its display **stale** and keeps showing the last state (PRD FR-MOB-8, FR-CON-8). On the next success it refreshes |

The full state is small at demo scale (6 hosts), so sending the whole state on a change is simpler and safe.

