## 13. Web Application Design

### 13.1 One Project, Two Experiences

The same React project serves the 3D console (desktop browser) and the mobile app (phone-sized, installed as a PWA). Both use the shared API client and polling hook in `shared/` (LEAD-CORE), so both always show the same state. The built project is served by the Flask app under `/app/` (DD-12); during development the Vite server forwards API calls to the Flask app so cookies work.

### 13.2 Screens

| Area | Screens |
|---|---|
| Mobile (GRAPH) | Mock login or onboarding; home dashboard with threat level; alert feed; alert and simplified 2D attack detail; Precog forecast view with Contain; history and reports |
| Console (VIZ) | 3D graph; selected node and alert detail; forecast cones; accuracy counter; containment result |
| Charts (VIZ) | Forecast accuracy over time; alert volume or severity over time |
| Push (VIZ) | Notification permission, token registration, notification display |

### 13.3 Login

The web app signs in through `POST /api/x/login` with the demo account; the session cookie then covers every call. A 401 from any add-on endpoint sends the user back to the login screen. NIDS's own page and its redirect-style behaviour are not used by the product's screens.

### 13.4 3D Console (VIZ)

| Item | Design |
|---|---|
| Library | `vasturiano/3d-force-graph`, built on Three.js (verified in the VIZ guide) |
| Nodes | Colour **and** shape or label change by state (not colour alone) |
| Propagation | New compromised nodes and connections animate in as polling brings changes |
| Forecast cones | Custom 3D objects from the compromised host toward each target; width and opacity scale with confidence |
| Counter | Shows session accuracy from `session_metrics` |
| After Contain | The node changes to contained and the cone to it is removed; the chain is shown ending early |
| Stale | A visible marker when polling fails |

### 13.5 Mobile App as PWA (GRAPH)

| Item | Design |
|---|---|
| Installability | A web app manifest and a service worker, scope `/app/`; HTTPS when hosted |
| Layout | Phone-first, dark, high-contrast, SOC style |
| Offline tolerance | The last received state is kept and shown with a stale marker when polling fails |
| Contain | One tap opens a confirmation, then calls propose and confirm in turn |

### 13.6 Charts (VIZ)

Chart.js components receive data through properties that match `session_metrics`, readable at phone width, and exposed through the charts area's entry file so mobile screens can import them without touching VIZ's folder.

### 13.7 Push Notifications (VIZ)

| Step | Design |
|---|---|
| 1 | The installed app asks for notification permission |
| 2 | Firebase returns a token; the app sends it to `POST /api/x/push/register` |
| 3 | When a new security event has severity `HIGH` (Proposed threshold), the backend sends a Firebase notification |
| 4 | A service worker shows the notification when the app is closed |
| Fallback | If push is unavailable, the in-app feed still shows every alert |
| Secrets | Firebase private keys live only in environment variables and are never committed |

