## 14. Containment and Re-evaluation Design

### 14.1 Containment Action (VIZ) — FR-RSP

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| 1 | A client calls `propose` for a target host |
| 2 | The action is stored as `pending_confirmation` and a confirmation screen is shown |
| 3 | On `confirm`, the host's state becomes `contained`, an audit entry is written and the version number goes up so clients see it on their next poll |
| 4 | Nothing real is changed anywhere: no firewall, no network, no device |

The Wazuh Active Response approach is a design pattern reference only.

### 14.2 Host State Machine

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> normal
    normal --> compromised: confirmed compromise
    normal --> contained: contained pre-emptively
    compromised --> contained: contained after compromise
    contained --> normal: replay restart
    compromised --> normal: replay restart
```

### 14.3 Re-evaluation

After a containment is executed the replay resumes. The next attempt toward the contained host is marked `blocked`, the graph records no new edge to it, the forecast is resolved as a blocked hit (Section 9.5), and a `reeval_result` states whether the attack progressed and whether the chain ended early. If the attack progressed elsewhere, the result says so and the loop continues with a new forecast.

