## 15. Security and Ethics Design

| # | Measure | Implements |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | No scanning, exploitation or attack tool runs anywhere; all attack behaviour is scripted data or an uploaded PCAP file | NFR-SEC-1, NFR-SEC-2 |
| 2 | NIDS's live sniffer is never started | FR-ING-10 |
| 3 | Containment changes only in-memory state and a log entry | NFR-SEC-3 |
| 4 | The propose and confirm flow cannot be skipped | NFR-SEC-6, DD-10 |
| 5 | The Flask secret key, the admin password and any tokens live in environment variables; `.env` is in `.gitignore`; only `.env.example` with names is committed. The hardcoded values in NIDS (secret key and the passwords in its Compose file) are replaced or unused | NFR-SEC-5, FR-API-8 |
| 6 | The demo login uses one account whose password is not reused anywhere else; NIDS's published default password is changed | NFR-SEC-4 |
| 7 | The hosted app uses HTTPS | Required for install and push |
| 8 | **Simulated threat intelligence is never shown as real.** NIDS's actor names (for example APT29), ASN and location values are generated from ports and hashes; the screens either hide them or label them clearly as simulated | P10 |
| 9 | Audit and log files never hold secrets or real personal data | FR-EVT-4 |
| 10 | Each reused repository's licence is recorded: NIDS's MIT licence and copyright notice stay with its copy; the Precog repository's permission email is kept and its paper cited | NFR-MNT-7 |
| 11 | The paper's methodology states that no live attacks were run | NFR-SEC-7 |

